(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["test12_1A"] = [{"id":"dc043599","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Most scientists agree that the moon was likely formed after a collision between Earth and a large planet named Theia. This collision likely created a huge debris field, made up of material from both Earth and Theia. Based on models of this event, scientists believe that the moon was formed from this debris over the course of thousands of years. Text 2 Researchers from NASA’s Ames Research Center used a computer to model how the moon could have formed. Although simulations of the moon’s formation have been done in the past, the team from NASA ran simulations that were much more detailed. They found that the formation of the moon was likely not a slow process that took many years. Instead, it’s probable that the moon’s formation happened immediately after impact, taking just a few hours.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Most scientists agree that the moon was likely formed after a collision between Earth and a large planet named Theia. This collision likely","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"created a huge debris field, made up of material from both Earth and Theia. Based on models of this event, scientists believe that the moon was","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"formed from this debris over the course of thousands of years.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best describes a difference in how the author of Text 1 and the author of Text 2 view the evidence for the formation of the moon?","choices":["The author of Text 1 argues that the formation of the moon occurred much earlier than the author of Text 2 argues.","The author of Text 1 suggests there is more evidence confirming the existence of Theia than the author of Text 2 suggests.","The author of Text 1 claims that the moon’s surface is more similar to Earth’s surface than the author of Text 2 claims.","The author of Text 1 believes that the moon formed more slowly than the author of Text 2 believes."]},{"id":"6977d22b","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Ecologists have long wondered how thousands of microscopic phytoplankton species can live together near ocean surfaces competing for the same resources. According to conventional wisdom, one species should emerge after outcompeting the rest. So why do so many species remain? Ecologists many efforts to explain this phenomenon still haven’t uncovered a satisfactory explanation. Text 2 Ecologist Michael Behrenfeld and colleagues have connected phytoplankton’s diversity to their microscopic size. Because these organisms are so tiny, they are spaced relatively far apart from each other in ocean water and, moreover, experience that water as a relatively dense substance. This in turn makes it hard for them to move around and interact with one another. Therefore, says Behrenfeld’s team, direct competition among phytoplankton probably happens much less than previously thought.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Ecologists have long wondered how thousands of microscopic phytoplankton species can live together near ocean surfaces competing for the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"same resources. According to conventional wisdom, one species should emerge after outcompeting the rest. So why do so many species","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"remain? Ecologists’ many efforts to explain this phenomenon still haven’t uncovered a satisfactory explanation.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Ecologist Michael Behrenfeld and colleagues have connected phytoplankton’s diversity to their microscopic size. Because these organisms are","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"so tiny, they are spaced relatively far apart from each other in ocean water and, moreover, experience that water as a relatively dense substance.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"This in turn makes it hard for them to move around and interact with one another. Therefore, says Behrenfeld’s team, direct competition among","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"phytoplankton probably happens much less than previously thought.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, how would Behrenfeld and colleagues (Text 2) most likely respond to the conventional wisdom discussed in Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that it is based on a misconception about phytoplankton species competing with one another","By asserting that it fails to recognize that routine replenishment of ocean nutrients prevents competition between phytoplankton species","By suggesting that their own findings help clarify how phytoplankton species are able to compete with larger organisms","By recommending that more ecologists focus their research on how competition among phytoplankton species is increased with water density"],"underline":"conventional wisdom,","underline_segments":["conventional wisdom,"]},{"id":"a49500cc","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Predatory animals differ widely in how they ______ food for their young. Some leave dead prey nearby for their young to consume, some bring live prey to their young, and some feed their young directly from their own mouths.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Predatory animals differ widely in how they ______ food for their young. Some leave dead prey nearby for their young to consume, some bring live","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"prey to their young, and some feed their young directly from their own mouths.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["avoid","guess","provide","describe"]},{"id":"05575bca","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The following text is adapted from Lewis Carroll’s 1871 novel Through the Looking-Glass, and What Alice Found There. Alice, a child, is talking to her cat. “Do you hear the snow against the window-panes, Kitty? How nice and soft it sounds! Just as if someone was kissing the window all over outside.” As used in the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Lewis Carroll’s 1871 novel Through the Looking-Glass, and What Alice Found There. Alice, a child, is talking to","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"her cat.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"“Do you hear the snow against the window-panes, Kitty? How nice and soft it sounds! Just as if someone was kissing the window","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"all over outside.”","indent":2}],"question":"As used in the text, what does the word “soft” most nearly mean?","choices":["Gentle","Sensitive","Shapeless","Bland"],"underline":"soft","underline_segments":["soft"]},{"id":"0b5ecf0e","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"During Rome’s republican period, which ended in the first century BCE, libraries were predominantly owned by wealthy individuals who tightly controlled access to their book collections. The first public library became available in Rome in 28 BCE and was soon followed by one commissioned by Emperor Augustus. As modern scholar Fabio Fernandes notes, however, these two traditions aren’t as distinct as they seem, as both the emperor and the private library owners viewed their libraries as extensions of their personal patronage, just on vastly differing scales.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"During Rome’s republican period, which ended in the first century BCE, libraries were predominantly owned by wealthy individuals who tightly","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"controlled access to their book collections. The first public library became available in Rome in 28 BCE and was soon followed by one","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"commissioned by Emperor Augustus. As modern scholar Fabio Fernandes notes, however, these two traditions aren’t as distinct as they seem,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"as both the emperor and the private library owners viewed their libraries as extensions of their personal patronage, just on vastly differing","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"scales.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To give a brief overview of public access to libraries throughout Rome’s republican period","To contend that early imperial leaders in Rome wielded too much influence over libraries","To assert that private and early public libraries in ancient Rome had an essential similarity","To call into question the notion that private Roman libraries disappeared during the first century BCE"]},{"id":"b93428a6","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Animal researcher Amalia P.M. Bastos led a 2021 study about a wild kea parrot that used small stones as tools to preen its feathers. Skeptical colleagues had initially suggested to Bastos that the kea’s interactions with the stones might simply be ______, but Bastos and her team showed that the kea was using the stones deliberately.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Animal researcher Amalia P.M. Bastos led a 2021 study about a wild kea parrot that used small stones as tools to preen its feathers. Skeptical","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"colleagues had initially suggested to Bastos that the kea’s interactions with the stones might simply be ______, but Bastos and her team showed","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"that the kea was using the stones deliberately.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["intriguing","obvious","accidental","observable"]},{"id":"ab56a107","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Cross-Text Connections","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Cross-Text Connections","passage":"Text 1 Digital art, the use of digital technology to create or display images, isn’t really art at all. It doesn’t require as much skill as creating physical art. “Painting” with a tablet and stylus is much easier than using paint and a brush: the technology is doing most of the work. Text 2 The painting programs used to create digital art involve more than just pressing a few buttons. In addition to knowing the fundamentals of art, digital artists need to be familiar with sophisticated software. Many artists will start by drawing an image on paper before transforming the piece to a digital format, where they can apply a variety of colors and techniques that would otherwise require many different traditional tools.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Text 1","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Digital art, the use of digital technology to create or display images, isn’t really art at all. It doesn’t require as much skill as creating physical art.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"“Painting” with a tablet and stylus is much easier than using paint and a brush: the technology is doing most of the work.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Text 2","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"The painting programs used to create digital art involve more than just pressing a few buttons. In addition to knowing the fundamentals of art,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"digital artists need to be familiar with sophisticated software. Many artists will start by drawing an image on paper before transforming the piece","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"to a digital format, where they can apply a variety of colors and techniques that would otherwise require many different traditional tools.","indent":0}],"question":"Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the claims of the author of Text 1?","choices":["By arguing that a piece of art created digitally can still be displayed traditionally","By explaining that it’s actually much harder to use a tablet and stylus to create art than to use paint and a brush","By insisting that digital art requires artistic abilities and skill even if it employs less traditional tools","By admitting that most digital artists don’t think fundamental drawing skills are important"]},{"id":"c8603ed7","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Text Structure and Purpose","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Text Structure and Purpose","passage":"San Francisco is known for the colorful murals painted on many of its buildings. The densest collection of murals is found on Balmy Alley in the Mission District neighborhood. In the 1970s, Latina artists painted vivid scenes of community life on walls along this block. As the original murals have faded, later generations of artists have painted new ones over them. As a result, Balmy Alley has become a living showcase of San Francisco’s artistic spirit, with its murals reflecting changes in the cultural life of the city.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"San Francisco is known for the colorful murals painted on many of its buildings. The densest collection of murals is found on Balmy Alley in the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Mission District neighborhood. In the 1970s, Latina artists painted vivid scenes of community life on walls along this block. As the original","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"murals have faded, later generations of artists have painted new ones over them. As a result, Balmy Alley has become a living showcase of San","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Francisco’s artistic spirit, with its murals reflecting changes in the cultural life of the city.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main purpose of the text?","choices":["To compare the Balmy Alley murals to other murals in San Francisco","To offer an overview of the history and importance of the Balmy Alley murals","To urge people to protect the murals of San Francisco from decay","To describe the rise of mural painting in San Francisco beginning in the 1970s"]},{"id":"d73a908a","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"Believing that living in an impractical space can heighten awareness and even improve health, conceptual artists Madeline Gins and Shusaku Arakawa designed an apartment building in Japan to be more fanciful than functional. A kitchen counter is chest-high on one side and knee-high on the other; a ceiling has a door to nowhere. The effect is disorienting but invigorating: after four years there, filmmaker Nobu Yamaoka reported significant health benefits.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Believing that living in an impractical space can heighten awareness and even improve health, conceptual artists Madeline Gins and Shusaku","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Arakawa designed an apartment building in Japan to be more fanciful than functional. A kitchen counter is chest-high on one side and knee-high","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"on the other; a ceiling has a door to nowhere. The effect is disorienting but invigorating: after four years there, filmmaker Nobu Yamaoka reported","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"significant health benefits.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Although inhabiting a home surrounded by fanciful features such as those designed by Gins and Arakawa can be rejuvenating, it is unsustainable.","Designing disorienting spaces like those in the Gins and Arakawa building is the most effective way to create a physically stimulating environment.","As a filmmaker, Yamaoka has long supported the designs of conceptual artists such as Gins and Arakawa.","Although impractical, the design of the apartment building by Gins and Arakawa may improve the well-being of the building’s residents."]},{"id":"3f236877","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"Ratified by more than 90 countries, the Nagoya Protocol is an international agreement ensuring that Indigenous communities are compensated when their agricultural resources and knowledge of wild plants and animals are utilized by agricultural corporations. However, the protocol has shortcomings. For example, it allows corporations to insist that their agreements with communities to conduct research on the commercial uses of the communities’ resources and knowledge remain confidential. Therefore, some Indigenous advocates express concern that the protocol may have the unintended effect of ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Ratified by more than 90 countries, the Nagoya Protocol is an international agreement ensuring that Indigenous communities are compensated","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"when their agricultural resources and knowledge of wild plants and animals are utilized by agricultural corporations. However, the protocol has","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"shortcomings. For example, it allows corporations to insist that their agreements with communities to conduct research on the commercial uses","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"of the communities’ resources and knowledge remain confidential. Therefore, some Indigenous advocates express concern that the protocol","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"may have the unintended effect of ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["diminishing the monetary reward that corporations might derive from their agreements with Indigenous communities.","limiting the research that corporations conduct on the resources of the Indigenous communities with which they have signed agreements.","preventing independent observers from determining whether the agreements guarantee equitable compensation for Indigenous communities.","discouraging Indigenous communities from learning new methods for harvesting plants and animals from their corporate partners."]},{"id":"baef99a5","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"The following text is adapted from Oscar Wilde’s 1891 novel The Picture of Dorian Gray. Dorian Gray is taking his first look at a portrait that Hallward has painted of him. Dorian passed listlessly in front of his picture and turned towards it. When he saw it he drew back, and his cheeks flushed for a moment with pleasure. A look of joy came into his eyes, as if he had recognized himself for the first time. He stood there motionless and in wonder, dimly conscious that Hallward was speaking to him, but not catching the meaning of his words. The sense of his own beauty came on him like a revelation. He had never felt it before. According to the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Oscar Wilde’s 1891 novel The Picture of Dorian Gray. Dorian Gray is taking his first look at a portrait that","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Hallward has painted of him.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Dorian passed listlessly in front of his picture and turned towards it. When he saw it he drew back, and his cheeks flushed for a moment with","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"pleasure. A look of joy came into his eyes, as if he had recognized himself for the first time. He stood there motionless and in wonder, dimly","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"conscious that Hallward was speaking to him, but not catching the meaning of his words. The sense of his own beauty came on him like a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"revelation. He had never felt it before.","indent":0}],"question":"According to the text, what is true about Dorian?","choices":["He wants to know Hallward’s opinion of the portrait.","He is delighted by what he sees in the portrait.","He prefers portraits to other types of paintings.","He is uncertain of Hallward’s talent as an artist."]},{"id":"c6b470bb","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"“Odalie” is an 1899 short story by Alice Dunbar-Nelson. In the story, a young woman named Odalie attends the annual Mardi Gras carnival in New Orleans, where she lives with her guardian Tante Louise. Dunbar-Nelson portrays Odalie as eager to escape the monotony of her everyday life: ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"“Odalie” is an 1899 short story by Alice Dunbar-Nelson. In the story, a young woman named Odalie attends the annual Mardi Gras carnival in New","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Orleans, where she lives with her guardian Tante Louise. Dunbar-Nelson portrays Odalie as eager to escape the monotony of her everyday life:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"______","indent":0}],"question":"Which quotation from “Odalie” most effectively illustrates the claim?","choices":["“Mardi Gras was a tiresome day, after all, she sighed, and Tante Louise agreed with her for once.”","“In the old French house on Royal Street, with its quaint windows and Spanish courtyard green and cool, and made musical by the plashing of the fountain and the trill of caged birds, lived Odalie in convent-like seclusion.”","“When one is shut up in a great French house with a grim sleepy tante and no companions of one’s own age, life becomes a dull thing, and one is ready for any new sensation.”","“It was Mardi Gras day at last, and early through her window Odalie could hear the jingle of folly bells on the [participants’] costumes, the tinkle of music, and the echoing strains of songs.”"]},{"id":"1755eaf0","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"Like many other genera of wild bees, bumblebees have in recent decades experienced population collapse caused by, among other factors, habitat destruction and climate variation. Bumblebees are also one of the most researched bee genera, second only to honeybees. As a result, ecologists have gained much of their insight about wild-bee declines from bumblebees. In a 2021 paper, zoologist Guillaume Ghisbain notes that bumblebees are among the relatively few wild-bee genera that display social behaviors and dietary generalism (ability to obtain nectar and pollen from a diversity of plant species), two traits that are associated with increased resilience to some specific environmental changes. Ghisbain therefore contends that ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Like many other genera of wild bees, bumblebees have in recent decades experienced population collapse caused by, among other factors,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"habitat destruction and climate variation. Bumblebees are also one of the most researched bee genera, second only to honeybees. As a result,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"ecologists have gained much of their insight about wild-bee declines from bumblebees. In a 2021 paper, zoologist Guillaume Ghisbain notes that","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"bumblebees are among the relatively few wild-bee genera that display social behaviors and dietary generalism (ability to obtain nectar and pollen","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"from a diversity of plant species), two traits that are associated with increased resilience to some specific environmental changes. Ghisbain","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"therefore contends that ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["although bumblebees and many other wild bees have experienced similar population declines in the past, compared with other wild bees, bumblebees are likely at greater risk of being harmed by climate variation than by habitat destruction.","although bumblebees have been more extensively studied than most wild bees, researchers should not use bumblebees to draw conclusions about the decline of other wild bees, even ones with feeding patterns and levels of sociability that are similar to those of bumblebees.","because bumblebees and other bees with generalist diets are less negatively affected by environmental stress than bees with specialized diets are, they are less likely to experience major population changes in the future than bees with specialized diets are.","because the responses of bumblebees and other wild bees to environmental threats are not always comparable, researchers need to exercise caution when extrapolating information about wild-bee population declines from bumblebees."]},{"id":"b69d821d","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"The following text is Vita Sackville-West’s circa 1920 poem “Evening.” Spars are ships masts, moorings are ropes that hold docked ships in place, and a riding-light is a light that a ship shines when it is anchored. When little lights in little ports come out, Quivering down through water with the stars, And all the fishing fleet of slender spars Range at their moorings, veer with tide about; When race of wind is stilled and sails are furled, And underneath our single riding-light The curve of black-ribbed deck gleams palely white, And slumbrous waters pool a slumbrous world; — Then, and then only, have I thought how sweet Old age might sink upon a windy youth, Quiet beneath the riding-light of truth, Weathered through storms, and gracious in retreat.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is Vita Sackville-West’s circa 1920 poem “Evening.” Spars are ships’ masts, moorings are ropes that hold docked ships in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"place, and a riding-light is a light that a ship shines when it is anchored.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"When little lights in little ports come out,","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Quivering down through water with the stars,","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"And all the fishing fleet of slender spars","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Range at their moorings, veer with tide about;","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"When race of wind is stilled and sails are furled,","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"And underneath our single riding-light","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"The curve of black-ribbed deck gleams palely white,","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"And slumbrous waters pool a slumbrous world;","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"—Then, and then only, have I thought how sweet","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Old age might sink upon a windy youth,","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Quiet beneath the riding-light of truth,","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Weathered through storms, and gracious in retreat.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The tranquility of a port in the evening can incline a person to appreciate the stillness of old age.","The difficulty of bringing a ship into port is apt training for dealing with the types of struggles encountered in old age.","A person who leads a long life that is varied and active may find it difficult to stay in a calm place.","The contrast between the peacefulness of a port at night and its activity during the day reflects the contrast between the calm of old age and the vibrancy of youth."]},{"id":"881ba6f1","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"Eighteenth-century economist Adam Smith is famed for his metaphor of the invisible hand, which he putatively used to illustrate a robust model of how individuals produce aggregate benefits by pursuing their own economic interests. Note putatively : as Gavin Kennedy has shown, Smith deploys this metaphor only once in his economic writings — to make a narrow point about the then-dominant economic theory of mercantilism — and it was largely ignored until some twentieth-century economists eager to secure an intellectual pedigree for their views elevated it to a fully- fledged paradigm.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Eighteenth-century economist Adam Smith is famed for his metaphor of the invisible hand, which he putatively used to illustrate a robust model","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"of how individuals produce aggregate benefits by pursuing their own economic interests. Note “putatively”: as Gavin Kennedy has shown, Smith","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"deploys this metaphor only once in his economic writings—to make a narrow point about the then-dominant economic theory of mercantilism—","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"and it was largely ignored until some twentieth-century economists eager to secure an intellectual pedigree for their views elevated it to a fully-","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"fledged paradigm.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["Although Smith is famed for his metaphor of the invisible hand, the metaphor was largely ignored until economists in the twentieth century came to realize that the metaphor was a robust model that anticipated their own views.","Some twentieth-century economists gave Smith’s metaphor of the invisible hand a significance it does not have in Smith’s work, but it is nevertheless a useful model of how individuals produce aggregate benefits by pursuing their own economic interests.","Smith’s metaphor of the invisible hand has been interpreted as a model of how individuals acting in their own interest produce aggregate benefits, but it was intended as a subtle critique of the economic theory of mercantilism.","The reputation of Smith’s metaphor of the invisible hand is not due to the importance of the metaphor in Smith’s work but rather to the promotion of the metaphor by some later economists for their own ends."]},{"id":"67614549","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"After winning the 1860 presidential election, Abraham Lincoln appointed Edward Bates, Salmon P. Chase, and William H. Seward to his cabinet. Lincoln’s decision was surprising, since each of these men had run against him, but historians have praised it, noting that Lincoln ______ his rivals diverse talents to strengthen his administration.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"After winning the 1860 presidential election, Abraham Lincoln appointed Edward Bates, Salmon P. Chase, and William H. Seward to his cabinet.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Lincoln’s decision was surprising, since each of these men had run against him, but historians have praised it, noting that Lincoln ______ his","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"rivals’ diverse talents to strengthen his administration.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["has leveraged","is leveraging","will leverage","leveraged"]},{"id":"c15069eb","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Latin America is known to have dozens, if not hundreds, of popular dance forms. Only five of these dances are included in international ballroom dance ______ rumba, samba, cha-cha-cha, paso doble, and jive — the last of which is grouped with the other Latin dances despite not having Latin roots.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Latin America is known to have dozens, if not hundreds, of popular dance forms. Only five of these dances are included in international ballroom","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"dance ______ rumba, samba, cha-cha-cha, paso doble, and jive—the last of which is grouped with the other Latin dances despite not having Latin","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"roots.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["competitions, however:","competitions, however,","competitions, however;","competitions; however,"]},{"id":"f4fd123c","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"The African Games Co-production Market, one of over 180 annual international conferences supporting video game development, ______ the growth of the African gaming industry by helping start-up studios in Africa find partners.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The African Games Co-production Market, one of over 180 annual international conferences supporting video game development, ______ the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"growth of the African gaming industry by helping start-up studios in Africa find partners.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["promote","are promoting","promotes","have promoted"]},{"id":"ac5536c1","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"Beatrix Potter is perhaps best known for writing and illustrating children’s books such as The Tale of Peter Rabbit (1902), but she also dedicated herself to mycology, the study of ______ more than 350 paintings of the fungal species she observed in nature and submitting her research on spore germination to the Linnean Society of London.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Beatrix Potter is perhaps best known for writing and illustrating children’s books such as The Tale of Peter Rabbit (1902), but she also dedicated","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"herself to mycology, the study of ______ more than 350 paintings of the fungal species she observed in nature and submitting her research on","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"spore germination to the Linnean Society of London.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["fungi; producing","fungi. Producing","fungi producing","fungi, producing"]},{"id":"a153ad6a","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"While light is known as one of the fastest-moving substances, it slows down when passing through some types of matter. One such type of matter is a form of cooled, condensed gas called a Bose-Einstein condensate ______ Dutch physicist Lene Hau famously used a BEC to slow a beam of light to a complete halt.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While light is known as one of the fastest-moving substances, it slows down when passing through some types of matter. One such type of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"matter is a form of cooled, condensed gas called a Bose-Einstein condensate ______ Dutch physicist Lene Hau famously used a BEC to slow a","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"beam of light to a complete halt.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["(BEC),","(BEC) and","(BEC);","(BEC)"]},{"id":"83898524","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"In addition to advocating for South America’s independence in two political treatises, the Cartagena Manifesto and the Letter from Jamaica, Simón Bolívar personally led armies against the Spanish, liberating three South American territories — New Granada (present-day Colombia and Panama), Venezuela, and Quito (present-day ______ from colonial rule.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In addition to advocating for South America’s independence in two political treatises, the Cartagena Manifesto and the Letter from Jamaica,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Simón Bolívar personally led armies against the Spanish, liberating three South American territories—New Granada (present-day Colombia and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Panama), Venezuela, and Quito (present-day ______ from colonial rule.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["Ecuador,)","Ecuador)","Ecuador),","Ecuador) —"]},{"id":"f114cbf0","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"A firefly uses specialized muscles to draw oxygen into its lower abdomen through narrow tubes, triggering a chemical reaction whereby the oxygen combines with chemicals in the firefly’s abdomen to produce a glow. ______ when the firefly stops drawing in oxygen, the reaction — and the glow — cease.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"A firefly uses specialized muscles to draw oxygen into its lower abdomen through narrow tubes, triggering a chemical reaction whereby the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"oxygen combines with chemicals in the firefly’s abdomen to produce a glow. ______ when the firefly stops drawing in oxygen, the reaction—and","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the glow—cease.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["For instance,","By contrast,","Specifically,","In conclusion,"]},{"id":"f33f0892","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"Although novels and poems are considered distinct literary forms, many authors have created hybrid works that incorporate elements of both. Bernardine Evaristo’s The Emperor’s Babe, ______ is a verse novel, a book-length narrative complete with characters and a plot but conveyed in short, crisp lines of poetry rather than prose.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Although novels and poems are considered distinct literary forms, many authors have created hybrid works that incorporate elements of both.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Bernardine Evaristo’s The Emperor’s Babe, ______ is a verse novel, a book-length narrative complete with characters and a plot but conveyed in","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"short, crisp lines of poetry rather than prose.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["by contrast,","consequently,","secondly,","for example,"]},{"id":"7f2781fd","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Certhidea olivacea is a perching bird that can be found on the Galápagos Island of Pinzón. Creagrus furcatus is a seabird that can be found on the Galápagos Island of Darwin. Conservation organizations evaluate the risk that species will become extinct in the near future. C. olivacea faces a high risk of extinction. C. furcatus faces little risk of becoming extinct in the near future. The student wants to compare the extinction risk faced by C. olivacea with that faced by C. furcatus.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Certhidea olivacea is a perching bird that can be found on the Galápagos Island of Pinzón.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Creagrus furcatus is a seabird that can be found on the Galápagos Island of Darwin.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Conservation organizations evaluate the risk that species will become extinct in the near future.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"C. olivacea faces a high risk of extinction.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"C. furcatus faces little risk of becoming extinct in the near future.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["According to conservation organizations, C. olivacea has a higher risk of becoming extinct in the near future than C. furcatus.","C. furcatus faces a high risk of extinction, while C. olivacea faces little risk of becoming extinct in the near future.","olivacea faces a high risk of extinction.","C. olivacea is a perching bird that faces a high risk of extinction."]},{"id":"1ccfcea4","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"The French directors Colas Koola and Vivien Mermet-Guyenet (known as Koola and Viv) founded the video game studio BlueTwelve. They also love cats. ______ they created an award-winning cat video game, Stray (2021), that lets the player explore a dystopian city from the perspective of a clever orange house cat.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The French directors Colas Koola and Vivien Mermet-Guyenet (known as Koola and Viv) founded the video game studio BlueTwelve. They also","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"love cats. ______ they created an award-winning cat video game, Stray (2021), that lets the player explore a dystopian city from the perspective of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"a clever orange house cat.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Intermittently,","On the other hand,","In fact,","Nevertheless,"]},{"id":"259d16ac","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: In 1859, the novel Adam Bede was published in England. According to the novel’s title page, the author’s name was George Eliot. George Eliot was widely assumed to be a pseudonym. A pseudonym is a fake name used to conceal an author’s identity. A woman named Mary Ann Evans later revealed herself as the novel’s real author. The student wants to identify the real author of Adam Bede.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"In 1859, the novel Adam Bede was published in England.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"According to the novel’s title page, the author’s name was George Eliot.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"George Eliot was widely assumed to be a pseudonym.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"A pseudonym is a fake name used to conceal an author’s identity.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"A woman named Mary Ann Evans later revealed herself as the novel’s real author.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["The real author of Adam Bede was Mary Ann Evans, who published the novel using the pseudonym George Eliot.","George Eliot, which Adam Bede’s title page indicated was the name of the novel’s author, was widely assumed to be a pseudonym.","The title page of the novel Adam Bede indicated that the author’s name was George Eliot.","A woman who had used a pseudonym to conceal her identity later revealed herself as the real author of Adam Bede."]},{"id":"5a2a4b36","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Pauline Hopkins (1859-1930) was an African American writer. In her career, she created many genre-defining stories. Her serialized novel Hagar’s Daughter was published from 1901 to 1902 in The Colored American Magazine. It is considered the first African American mystery novel. The student wants to introduce Pauline Hopkins to an audience unfamiliar with her career.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Pauline Hopkins (1859-1930) was an African American writer.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"In her career, she created many genre-defining stories.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Her serialized novel Hagar’s Daughter was published from 1901 to 1902 in The Colored American Magazine.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"It is considered the first African American mystery novel.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Pauline Hopkins is the author of Hagar’s Daughter, a serialized novel published from 1901 to 1902.","African American writer Pauline Hopkins created many genre-defining stories during her career, including the first African American mystery novel, Hagar’s Daughter.","Published in The Colored American Magazine from 1901 to 1902, Hagar’s Daughter is a serialized novel by Pauline Hopkins.","Hagar’s Daughter by Pauline Hopkins is considered the first African American mystery novel."]}];
